# Line-ending normalisation. Without this, Git on Windows (with the
# default `core.autocrlf=true`) silently converts LF → CRLF on
# checkout. That's fine for files that only ever run on Windows, but
# everything in this repo is shipped into Linux containers — and
# Linux `sh` chokes on \r in shell scripts with errors like
# "set: Illegal option -" or "command not found" on a line that
# looks perfectly normal in the editor.
#
# After changing this file, contributors with already-checked-out
# trees need to re-normalise:
#
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git status      # shows what got LF-fixed
#   git commit -m "Normalise line endings"
#
# (Or on Windows, `git rm --cached -r .` followed by
# `git reset --hard` does the same thing.)

# Default: let Git auto-detect text vs binary, but always store +
# check out with LF — even on Windows. Cross-platform projects with
# a Linux deploy target want this set.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit overrides for files that MUST be LF even if auto-detect
# guesses wrong. These run inside containers / CI runners:
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.bash       text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.js         text eol=lf
*.cjs        text eol=lf
*.mjs        text eol=lf
*.ts         text eol=lf
*.tsx        text eol=lf
*.env*       text eol=lf

# Files that are allowed to keep CRLF on Windows checkouts (rare,
# but listed to be explicit). None currently — placeholder.

# Binary files: never normalise, never diff line by line.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp3   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.zip   binary
*.tar   binary
*.gz    binary
